`courses/opencv-w-wizji-komputerowej-od-podstaw-do-sar/lessons/filtry-rozmywajace-sredni-gaussa-medianowy.json` — 6 sections (theory → theory → demo → code → quiz → sandbox), 12 min, module `m3`. Polish content per course conventions.
- **Theory 1** "Trzy strategie rozmywania" — frames mean/Gaussian/median; inline Wikimedia salt-and-pepper image with descriptive alt text.
- **Theory 2** "Jądro Gaussa — wzór, rola σ i separowalność" — derives the kernel formula (per research note: "Derive the Gaussian-kernel formula in Module 3 lesson 2"), the `k ≈ 6σ + 1` rule, and the separability cost argument.
- **Demo (gauss)** — interactive σ slider on `/demo-images/cameraman.jpg`, σ ∈ [0, 6], default 2. The course's single allowed `gauss` demo, used here per the research notes.
- **Code** — `gaussian_kernel(ksize, sigma)` from scratch; 4 tests (1 visible smoke + 3 hidden), full reference solution.
- **Quiz** — salt-and-pepper distractor set drawn from the "Common misconceptions" list (the *"median is just a slow blur"* and *"all blurs are equivalent"* myths).
- **Sandbox** — side-by-side `cv2.blur` / `cv2.GaussianBlur` / `cv2.medianBlur` on a synthetic noisy gradient.
5 lesson-level sources (Wikipedia × 2, official OpenCV docs, Szeliski, Gonzalez & Woods) — all stable hosts; books carry author + year. Two theory sections + the quiz carry section-level `sources` for the references they specifically lean on.
